1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,960
Welcome to Rashi in Brief, where each week

2
00:00:03,280 --> 00:00:05,612
we explore the Torah portion through the eyes

3
00:00:05,665 --> 00:00:09,772
of the great medieval commentator, Rabbi Shlomo Yitzchaki,

4
00:00:09,825 --> 00:00:11,212
known as Rashi.

5
00:00:11,372 --> 00:00:14,572
This week's portion is Ki Tavo, found in

6
00:00:14,625 --> 00:00:18,652
Deuteronomy chapters twenty-six through twenty-nine, and it's a

7
00:00:18,712 --> 00:00:21,292
portion that takes us from beautiful rituals of

8
00:00:21,364 --> 00:00:24,092
gratitude to some of the most intense and

9
00:00:24,172 --> 00:00:29,548
sobering passages in all of Torah.Ki Tavo opens

10
00:00:29,612 --> 00:00:32,108
with two lovely ceremonial moments.

11
00:00:32,295 --> 00:00:35,868
First, there's the mitzvah of bikkurim, or first

12
00:00:35,937 --> 00:00:38,908
fruits, where a farmer brings the earliest produce

13
00:00:38,935 --> 00:00:41,148
of his harvest to the Temple and makes

14
00:00:41,228 --> 00:00:45,068
a declaration of gratitude, recounting Israel's journey from

15
00:00:45,088 --> 00:00:47,468
the days of the patriarchs through slavery in

16
00:00:47,548 --> 00:00:49,868
Egypt and into the Promised Land.

17
00:00:50,124 --> 00:00:55,228
Then comes vidui maaser, the tithe declaration, where

18
00:00:55,268 --> 00:00:58,268
a person affirms that they've properly distributed all

19
00:00:58,308 --> 00:00:59,228
their tithes.

20
00:00:59,481 --> 00:01:03,788
After these rituals, the portion shifts dramatically.

21
00:01:03,975 --> 00:01:07,308
Moses instructs the people about a ceremony at

22
00:01:07,348 --> 00:01:10,508
Mount Gerizim and Mount Ebal, where blessings and

23
00:01:10,599 --> 00:01:12,188
curses will be proclaimed.

24
00:01:12,428 --> 00:01:16,428
And then comes the Tochacha, the long, harrowing

25
00:01:16,488 --> 00:01:18,908
litany of curses that will befall the nation

26
00:01:19,041 --> 00:01:21,148
if they abandon God's commandments.

27
00:01:21,495 --> 00:01:25,708
Rashi, characteristically, is drawn to the fine details

28
00:01:25,761 --> 00:01:26,588
in all of this.

29
00:01:26,855 --> 00:01:30,668
He asks why specific words appear, what legal

30
00:01:30,748 --> 00:01:34,188
rules are embedded in seemingly simple phrases, and

31
00:01:34,248 --> 00:01:36,588
how the structure of the text reveals layers

32
00:01:36,641 --> 00:01:40,918
of meaning that we might otherwise miss.Let's begin

33
00:01:40,950 --> 00:01:43,078
with the very opening of the portion.

34
00:01:43,198 --> 00:01:46,199
The Torah says, "And it shall be, when

35
00:01:46,219 --> 00:01:48,119
you come into the land and possess it

36
00:01:48,159 --> 00:01:50,599
and settle therein, then you shall take of

37
00:01:50,619 --> 00:01:52,439
the first of all the fruit of the

38
00:01:52,519 --> 00:01:56,119
ground." Rashi zeroes in on the phrase "and

39
00:01:56,179 --> 00:01:59,319
possess it and settle therein." Why does the

40
00:01:59,359 --> 00:02:01,562
Torah add these extra words?

41
00:02:01,749 --> 00:02:04,602
He explains that this teaches us the Israelites

42
00:02:04,650 --> 00:02:07,562
were not obligated to bring first fruits until

43
00:02:07,594 --> 00:02:10,602
they had both conquered and divided the land.

44
00:02:10,742 --> 00:02:12,842
The mitzvah doesn't kick in the moment they

45
00:02:12,909 --> 00:02:14,162
cross the Jordan.

46
00:02:14,255 --> 00:02:16,642
It requires full settlement.

47
00:02:16,746 --> 00:02:19,322
Then Rashi turns to the phrase "of the

48
00:02:19,415 --> 00:02:22,762
first," noting that it says "of the first"

49
00:02:22,802 --> 00:02:26,362
and not "all the first." Not every fruit

50
00:02:26,442 --> 00:02:27,602
qualifies.

51
00:02:27,690 --> 00:02:29,962
Only the seven species for which the Land

52
00:02:30,015 --> 00:02:34,762
of Israel is praised: wheat, barley, grapes, figs,

53
00:02:34,842 --> 00:02:37,562
pomegranates, olives, and dates.

54
00:02:37,749 --> 00:02:41,322
Rashi makes this connection through a textual parallel.

55
00:02:41,422 --> 00:02:44,602
The word "land" appears both here and in

56
00:02:44,642 --> 00:02:47,642
the verse listing those seven species in Deuteronomy

57
00:02:47,702 --> 00:02:50,522
chapter eight, linking the two passages.

58
00:02:50,662 --> 00:02:54,042
And even among those seven species, not every

59
00:02:54,122 --> 00:02:56,362
ripe fruit becomes bikkurim.

60
00:02:56,549 --> 00:02:59,162
Rashi explains that when a person goes into

61
00:02:59,189 --> 00:03:01,882
their field and sees the very first fig

62
00:03:01,914 --> 00:03:04,282
that has ripened, they tie a string around

63
00:03:04,335 --> 00:03:07,722
it and say, "This is bikkurim." It's a

64
00:03:07,802 --> 00:03:12,042
small, intimate gesture, one person marking one fruit,

65
00:03:12,062 --> 00:03:14,762
and it captures something profound about how gratitude

66
00:03:14,831 --> 00:03:18,894
begins with noticing.Next, let's look at the declaration

67
00:03:18,934 --> 00:03:21,694
the farmer makes when bringing the first fruits.

68
00:03:21,854 --> 00:03:25,134
He begins with the famous words "Arami oved

69
00:03:25,234 --> 00:03:28,894
avi," which is often translated as "a wandering

70
00:03:28,974 --> 00:03:32,334
Aramean was my father." But Rashi reads it

71
00:03:32,430 --> 00:03:33,694
very differently.

72
00:03:33,881 --> 00:03:37,534
He translates it as "an Aramean destroyed my

73
00:03:37,603 --> 00:03:41,214
father," referring to Laban the Aramean, who pursued

74
00:03:41,294 --> 00:03:44,014
Jacob and wished to wipe out Jacob's entire

75
00:03:44,094 --> 00:03:44,814
family.

76
00:03:45,027 --> 00:03:48,414
Rashi says that even though Laban didn't succeed,

77
00:03:48,494 --> 00:03:50,974
God considers the intention of the nations as

78
00:03:51,025 --> 00:03:52,414
equivalent to the deed.

79
00:03:52,654 --> 00:03:56,895
So the Torah uses a past-tense verb, "destroyed,"

80
00:03:56,922 --> 00:03:58,655
as if it actually happened.

81
00:03:58,799 --> 00:04:02,175
This reading transforms the declaration from a vague

82
00:04:02,233 --> 00:04:06,015
historical reference into a sharp theological statement.

83
00:04:06,228 --> 00:04:09,695
Gratitude, Rashi is showing us, is rooted in

84
00:04:09,748 --> 00:04:12,335
an awareness of the dangers we have survived,

85
00:04:12,415 --> 00:04:16,565
even the ones that never fully materialized.Moving to

86
00:04:16,585 --> 00:04:20,005
the tithe declaration in chapter twenty-six, the person

87
00:04:20,069 --> 00:04:23,285
says before God, "I have removed the hallowed

88
00:04:23,342 --> 00:04:26,965
things from the house." Rashi asks what exactly

89
00:04:27,045 --> 00:04:29,205
are these "hallowed things"?

90
00:04:29,418 --> 00:04:32,485
He identifies them as the second tithe, which

91
00:04:32,538 --> 00:04:35,845
is called kodesh or holy, and also the

92
00:04:35,898 --> 00:04:38,165
fruit of a vineyard in its fourth year,

93
00:04:38,245 --> 00:04:40,805
which is likewise termed holy.

94
00:04:40,925 --> 00:04:43,445
The person is affirming that if they still

95
00:04:43,525 --> 00:04:46,325
had second tithes from the first two years

96
00:04:46,352 --> 00:04:49,205
of the sabbatical cycle sitting in their house,

97
00:04:49,221 --> 00:04:51,045
they have now properly brought them up to

98
00:04:51,117 --> 00:04:51,845
Jerusalem.

99
00:04:52,053 --> 00:04:55,765
Then the person continues, "I have also given

100
00:04:55,818 --> 00:04:59,125
to the Levite." Rashi says this refers to

101
00:04:59,165 --> 00:05:00,405
the first tithe.

102
00:05:00,605 --> 00:05:03,845
But then he catches the word "also," the

103
00:05:03,902 --> 00:05:07,925
Hebrew word "v'gam," and says the seemingly extra

104
00:05:08,053 --> 00:05:12,165
word comes to include terumah, the priestly portion,

105
00:05:12,285 --> 00:05:14,005
and first fruits as well.

106
00:05:14,352 --> 00:05:18,405
Rashi reveals an entire system of agricultural law

107
00:05:18,525 --> 00:05:21,445
encoded in what sounds like a simple personal

108
00:05:21,501 --> 00:05:22,165
statement.

109
00:05:22,352 --> 00:05:27,122
Every word carries legal weight.Now let's turn to

110
00:05:27,142 --> 00:05:27,922
the curses.

111
00:05:28,142 --> 00:05:31,282
One of the most striking moments in Rashi's

112
00:05:31,340 --> 00:05:34,402
commentary comes when he compares the curses here

113
00:05:34,455 --> 00:05:36,802
in Ki Tavo with the earlier set of

114
00:05:36,882 --> 00:05:41,042
curses in Leviticus, in the portion called Bechukotai.

115
00:05:41,202 --> 00:05:43,844
The Torah says here that the heavens will

116
00:05:43,901 --> 00:05:47,284
become like copper and the earth like iron,

117
00:05:47,324 --> 00:05:50,484
but in Leviticus it says the reverse: heavens

118
00:05:50,532 --> 00:05:53,204
like iron and earth like copper.

119
00:05:53,471 --> 00:05:56,724
Rashi explains that there is actually a meaningful

120
00:05:56,782 --> 00:05:57,684
difference.

121
00:05:57,817 --> 00:06:01,604
In Leviticus, heavens like iron means no moisture

122
00:06:01,684 --> 00:06:05,204
at all comes from the sky, total drought,

123
00:06:05,244 --> 00:06:08,404
while earth like copper means the ground sweats

124
00:06:08,464 --> 00:06:10,004
and rots the fruit.

125
00:06:10,116 --> 00:06:11,284
That's worse.

126
00:06:11,524 --> 00:06:15,684
Here in Ki Tavo, heavens like copper means

127
00:06:15,732 --> 00:06:19,124
they do exude some moisture, even if not

128
00:06:19,188 --> 00:06:22,484
real rain, so at least there's no catastrophic

129
00:06:22,544 --> 00:06:25,924
drought, and earth like iron means the ground

130
00:06:25,991 --> 00:06:29,284
doesn't sweat, so the fruit doesn't rot.

131
00:06:29,524 --> 00:06:33,764
It's still terrible, Rashi says, because neither scenario

132
00:06:33,835 --> 00:06:37,524
produces food, but Moses, speaking in his own

133
00:06:37,591 --> 00:06:40,884
voice here rather than quoting God directly, was

134
00:06:40,937 --> 00:06:43,444
slightly milder in his formulation.

135
00:06:43,791 --> 00:06:46,964
Rashi is teaching us something remarkable about the

136
00:06:47,033 --> 00:06:50,884
nature of prophecy and about Moses's compassion even

137
00:06:50,937 --> 00:06:55,841
in delivering the harshest warnings.Finally, there's a devastating

138
00:06:55,891 --> 00:06:58,405
passage about the siege, where the Torah says

139
00:06:58,437 --> 00:07:01,045
that a tender and delicate person will eat

140
00:07:01,085 --> 00:07:03,525
the flesh of his own children because of

141
00:07:03,545 --> 00:07:04,325
the famine.

142
00:07:04,618 --> 00:07:08,805
Rashi explains that the words "tender" and "delicate"

143
00:07:08,858 --> 00:07:12,485
describe the same person, someone so refined that

144
00:07:12,578 --> 00:07:15,685
under normal circumstances he would be sickened by

145
00:07:15,729 --> 00:07:17,125
anything unpleasant.

146
00:07:17,265 --> 00:07:20,325
Yet hunger will so overwhelm him that he

147
00:07:20,373 --> 00:07:23,365
will refuse to share even this terrible food

148
00:07:23,413 --> 00:07:25,205
with his surviving children.

149
00:07:25,498 --> 00:07:28,885
Rashi's reading forces us to confront the full

150
00:07:28,954 --> 00:07:31,685
horror of the curse, that it doesn't just

151
00:07:31,765 --> 00:07:35,365
destroy bodies but inverts the most basic human

152
00:07:35,445 --> 00:07:36,405
instincts.

153
00:07:36,565 --> 00:07:39,365
The person who was once the most compassionate

154
00:07:39,465 --> 00:07:43,205
becomes, under unbearable pressure, the most cruel.

155
00:07:43,498 --> 00:07:45,925
It's a warning about what can happen when

156
00:07:45,965 --> 00:07:49,045
a society collapses, and Rashi doesn't look away

157
00:07:49,093 --> 00:07:54,105
from it.Stepping back from these individual comments, we

158
00:07:54,145 --> 00:07:57,305
can see several threads running through Rashi's approach

159
00:07:57,358 --> 00:07:58,345
to Ki Tavo.

160
00:07:58,625 --> 00:08:01,945
One is his attention to the precise legal

161
00:08:02,007 --> 00:08:06,025
architecture hidden within poetic and narrative language.

162
00:08:06,225 --> 00:08:10,425
The first-fruits ceremony and the tithe declaration are

163
00:08:10,485 --> 00:08:14,745
not just emotional expressions; they are carefully constructed

164
00:08:14,838 --> 00:08:18,265
legal acts, and every word matters.

165
00:08:18,445 --> 00:08:23,385
Another theme is Rashi's sensitivity to gradations, whether

166
00:08:23,438 --> 00:08:25,945
in the severity of curses, the levels of

167
00:08:26,005 --> 00:08:29,385
holiness in different tithes, or the distinction between

168
00:08:29,485 --> 00:08:31,946
God's voice and Moses's voice.

169
00:08:32,226 --> 00:08:36,346
And perhaps most powerfully, Rashi shows us that

170
00:08:36,366 --> 00:08:39,308
the Torah's vision of blessing and curse is

171
00:08:39,408 --> 00:08:40,668
not abstract.

172
00:08:40,855 --> 00:08:43,948
It's rooted in daily life, in a fig

173
00:08:44,017 --> 00:08:46,908
marked with a string, in rain that falls

174
00:08:46,988 --> 00:08:50,348
but not enough, in the human heart pushed

175
00:08:50,417 --> 00:08:51,948
beyond its limits.

176
00:08:52,375 --> 00:08:55,791
Rashi reads the Torah as a text that

177
00:08:55,848 --> 00:08:59,231
speaks simultaneously to the legal mind, the moral

178
00:08:59,296 --> 00:09:05,081
conscience, and the human heart.Thank you so much

179
00:09:05,121 --> 00:09:07,561
for joining me on Rashi in Brief.

180
00:09:07,841 --> 00:09:10,761
Wishing you a meaningful week of learning, and

181
00:09:10,801 --> 00:09:12,041
I'll see you next time.

